Found by the secrets worker during k360 (2026-10-31), by running each command through bin/ocp with --pins-stdin on a fresh clone (keys.yaml, age.key.enc, kubeconfig.yaml committed, no .ocp/).

On a clone of an already-deployed cluster, these commands fail before PIN1 is even asked:
- ocp status, ocp kubeconfig, ocp node ls, ocp health, ocp provider ls, ocp deploy-robocop;
- ocp apply and ocp apply --dry-run when kubeconfig.yaml exists;
- by the worker's reading of the code, also node add/rm/trust-host, update, destroy, hetzner list.

These are the read-only commands a teammate runs first. They read .ocp/age.key directly, not through ensure_age_key, and die with a raw Path::Tiny error: `Error open (<) on '.ocp/age.key': No such file or directory`.

k360 (the write side) made the commands that go through ensure_age_key work on a clone (keys *, ssh, inject-key, apply without kubeconfig.yaml). This card is the read side.

Done =
- Find the shared read: likely OCP::Secrets' kubeconfig decrypt or identities reading age_key_file.
- Every listed command either unlocks the key the way ensure_age_key does (PIN1, from age.key.enc, cached in .ocp/age.key), or refuses with a diagnosis naming the fix, on STDERR, exit non-zero, with no Perl error text. Prefer the unlock, and say why if a command shouldn't.
- One regression test per command group on a clone fixture, red first, then a mutation check.

Lanes: ocp-secrets-worker for the shared read in OCP::Secrets. Command lanes only if a command needs its own change; list them in the note.

Scope note (main, 2026-10-31): 'ocp apply --dry-run when kubeconfig.yaml exists' is covered by k349's apply half (Apply/Drift.pm reconcile_components unlocks into memory before read_kubeconfig; test t/349-apply-dry-run-clone.t). The real 'ocp apply' run with kubeconfig.yaml stays here. If the shared read in OCP::Secrets learns to unlock, it must keep honouring an age_key already held in memory (_unlock_age_key returns early on defined age_key) so the dry run still writes nothing.

